export const questions = [
{"no":1,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:The atmosphere is heated more by incoming solar radiation than by terrestrial radiation.\nII. Statement-II:Carbon dioxide and other greenhouse gases in the atmosphere are good absorbers of long wave radiation.","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"D","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement-I is incorrect: The atmosphere is heated primarily by terrestrial radiation emitted by the Earth's surface, rather than directly by incoming shortwave solar radiation.\nThe Earth's surface absorbs much of the incoming solar radiation and subsequently emits longwave terrestrial radiation.\nStatement-II is correct: Greenhouse gases such as carbon dioxide, water vapour and methane absorb outgoing longwave radiation.\nThis absorption and re-emission of longwave radiation warms the atmosphere.\nTherefore, Statement-I is incorrect and Statement-II is correct.","revisionPoints":"Sun → shortwave radiation → mainly heats Earth's surface.\nEarth → longwave terrestrial radiation → heats atmosphere.\nGreenhouse gases are effective absorbers of longwave/infrared radiation.\nMajor greenhouse gases:\nWater vapour\nCO₂\nCH₄\nN₂O\nO₃\nQuick recall: Atmosphere is heated mainly from below, by terrestrial radiation."},
{"no":2,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:Thickness of the troposphere at the equator is much greater as compared to poles.\nII. Statement-II:At the equator, heat is transported to great heights by strong convectional currents.","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement-I is correct: The troposphere is considerably thicker over the equator than over the poles.\nAt the equator, intense solar heating causes strong convectional currents.\nWarm air rises to considerable heights, causing the upper boundary of the troposphere—the tropopause—to be higher.\nNear the poles, weaker heating and weaker convection result in a thinner troposphere.\nThus, Statement-II correctly explains Statement-I.","revisionPoints":"Troposphere thickness:\nEquator → about 16–18 km\nPoles → about 8 km\nStrong convection → higher tropopause.\nTroposphere contains most of the atmosphere's:\nWater vapour\nClouds\nWeather phenomena\nQuick recall: More heating + stronger convection → thicker troposphere."},
{"no":3,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Pyroclastic debris\n2. Ash and dust\n3. Nitrogen compounds\n4. Sulphur compounds\nHow many of the above are products of volcanic eruptions?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Pyroclastic debris is a major product of explosive volcanic eruptions.\nAsh and dust are fine volcanic materials ejected during eruptions.\nSulphur compounds, including sulphur dioxide and hydrogen sulphide, are important volcanic gases.\nNitrogen compounds, as listed here, are not generally considered a principal characteristic product of volcanic eruptions in this context.\nTherefore, three of the four listed items are products of volcanic eruptions.","revisionPoints":"Volcanic products include:\nLava\nAsh\nVolcanic dust\nPyroclastic debris\nVolcanic gases\nImportant volcanic gases:\nWater vapour\nCO₂\nSO₂\nHydrogen sulphide\nOther gases in smaller quantities\nPyroclastic material → fragmented volcanic material ejected during explosive eruptions."},
{"no":4,"text":"Which of the following is/are correct inference/inferences from isothermal maps in the month of January?\n1. The isotherms deviate to the north over the ocean and to the south over the continent.\n2. The presence of cold ocean currents, Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift make the North Atlantic Ocean colder and the isotherms bend towards the north.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: During January, the Northern Hemisphere is experiencing winter. Oceans retain heat longer than land, so temperatures over oceans are relatively higher.\nConsequently, isotherms tend to bend northward over oceans and southward over continents.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The Gulf Stream and North Atlantic Drift are warm ocean currents, not cold currents. They warm the North Atlantic region and cause isotherms to bend northward.\nTherefore, only Statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"January → Northern Hemisphere winter.\nOceans cool more slowly than continents.\nWarm ocean → isotherms bend poleward/northward.\nCold ocean → isotherms bend equatorward/southward.\nGulf Stream → warm current.\nNorth Atlantic Drift → continuation of warm North Atlantic circulation."},
{"no":5,"text":"Which of the following countries are well known as the two largest cocoa producers in the world?","A":"Algeria and Morocco","B":"Botswana and Namibia","C":"Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana","D":"Madagascar and Mozambique","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Economic Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Côte d'Ivoire (Ivory Coast) is the world's leading cocoa producer.\nGhana is another major global producer and is generally ranked among the world's largest cocoa-producing countries.\nCocoa cultivation is concentrated in the tropical regions where warm temperatures and adequate rainfall prevail.\nTherefore, Côte d'Ivoire and Ghana are the correct pair.","revisionPoints":"Côte d'Ivoire → world's leading cocoa producer.\nGhana → major cocoa producer.\nCocoa is primarily cultivated in the tropical belt.\nCocoa is an important raw material for chocolate production.\nWest Africa has a dominant position in global cocoa production."},
{"no":6,"text":"With reference to the Himalayan rivers joining the Ganga, downstream of Prayagraj from West to East, which one of the following sequences is correct?","A":"Ghaghara – Gomati – Gandak – Kosi","B":"Gomati – Ghaghara – Gandak – Kosi","C":"Ghaghara – Gomati – Kosi – Gandak","D":"Gomati – Ghaghara – Kosi – Gandak","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Moving from west to east downstream of Prayagraj:\nGomati joins the Ganga near Ghazipur.\nGhaghara joins the Ganga near Chhapra.\nGandak joins the Ganga near Patna.\nKosi joins the Ganga further east near Kursela.\nThus, the correct west-to-east sequence is:\nGomati → Ghaghara → Gandak → Kosi","revisionPoints":"Gomati → Uttar Pradesh → Ganga\nGhaghara → Nepal/Himalayan region → Ganga\nGandak → Nepal → Ganga\nKosi → Nepal/Tibet region → Ganga\nQuick recall: West → East = Gomati → Ghaghara → Gandak → Kosi."},
{"no":7,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:Rainfall is one of the reasons for weathering of rocks.\nII. Statement-II:Rain water contains carbon dioxide in solution.\nIII. Statement-III:Rain water contains atmospheric oxygen.","A":"Both Statement-II and Statement-III are correct and both explain Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-II and Statement-III are correct, but only one explains Statement-I.","C":"Only one of the Statements II and III is correct and that explains Statement-I.","D":"Neither Statement-II nor Statement-III is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement-I is correct: Rainfall contributes to the chemical and physical weathering of rocks.\nStatement-II is correct: Rainwater dissolves atmospheric carbon dioxide, forming weak carbonic acid. This promotes chemical weathering, particularly of carbonate rocks.\nStatement-III is correct: Rainwater also contains dissolved atmospheric oxygen, which facilitates oxidation reactions involved in weathering.\nThus, both dissolved CO₂ and oxygen contribute to the weathering of rocks.\nTherefore, (a) is correct.","revisionPoints":"Rainwater + CO₂ → weak carbonic acid.\nCarbonic acid → important in chemical weathering.\nDissolved oxygen → supports oxidation.\nWeathering types:\nPhysical/mechanical\nChemical\nBiological\nQuick recall: Rainfall accelerates weathering through water + CO₂ + O₂."},
{"no":8,"text":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Finland\n2. Germany\n3. Norway\n4. Russia\nHow many of the above countries have a border with the North Sea?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Germany has a coastline on the North Sea.\nNorway also has a coastline bordering the North Sea.\nFinland borders the Baltic Sea, specifically the Gulf of Bothnia and Gulf of Finland, not the North Sea.\nRussia has Baltic, Arctic and Pacific coastlines, but it does not have a North Sea coastline.\nTherefore, two countries—Germany and Norway—border the North Sea.","revisionPoints":"North Sea littoral countries include:\nUnited Kingdom\nNorway\nDenmark\nGermany\nNetherlands\nBelgium\nFrance\nFinland → Baltic Sea\nRussia → not North Sea\nQuick recall: North Sea lies between Great Britain and continental northwestern Europe."},
{"no":9,"text":"Consider the following information:\nWaterfall\nRegion\nRiver\n1. Dhuandhar\nMalwa\nNarmada\n2. Hundru\nChota Nagpur\nSubarnarekha\n3. Gersoppa\nWestern Ghats\nNetravati\nIn how many of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography (India)","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Row 1 is incorrect: Dhuandhar Falls is on the Narmada River near Jabalpur, but it is not correctly associated with the Malwa region in this context.\nRow 2 is correct: Hundru Falls is located in the Chota Nagpur Plateau and is formed by the Subarnarekha River.\nRow 3 is incorrect: Gersoppa Falls, also known as Jog Falls, is on the Sharavathi River, not the Netravati.\nTherefore, only one row is correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Hundru Falls → Subarnarekha → Chota Nagpur Plateau\nGersoppa/Jog Falls → Sharavathi → Karnataka\nDhuandhar Falls → Narmada → Madhya Pradesh\nUPSC often tests waterfalls through the combination:Waterfall + River + State/Region."},
{"no":10,"text":"Consider the following information:\nRegion\nMountain Range\nType\n1. Central Asia\nVosges\nFold mountain\n2. Europe\nAlps\nBlock mountain\n3. North America\nAppalachians\nFold mountain\n4. South America\nAndes\nFold mountain\nIn how many of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Row 1 is incorrect: The Vosges are in Europe, specifically France, and are classified as block mountains, not fold mountains of Central Asia.\nRow 2 is incorrect: The Alps are in Europe, but they are young fold mountains, not block mountains.\nRow 3 is correct: The Appalachian Mountains are in North America and are ancient fold mountains.\nRow 4 is correct: The Andes are in South America and are young fold mountains associated with the convergence of the Nazca and South American plates.\nHence, only two rows—3 and 4—are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Vosges → Europe → Block mountains\nAlps → Europe → Fold mountains\nAppalachians → North America → Fold mountains\nAndes → South America → Fold mountains\nMajor mountain types:\nFold mountains → compression/convergence\nBlock mountains → faulting\nVolcanic mountains → volcanic activity\nQuick recall: Alps + Appalachians + Andes → fold mountains."},
{"no":11,"text":"Consider the following airports:\n1. Donyi Polo Airport\n2. Kushinagar International Airport\n3. Vijayawada International Airport\nIn the recent past, which of the above have been constructed as Greenfield projects?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Infrastructure","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"A Greenfield airport is developed on a new site rather than by simply expanding an existing airport facility.\nDonyi Polo Airport, Itanagar was developed as a new Greenfield airport.\nKushinagar International Airport was also developed as a Greenfield airport.\nVijayawada International Airport was developed through a new airport project at Gannavaram, with a new terminal and associated infrastructure, and is treated under the Greenfield airport-development framework in the supplied key.\nTherefore, according to the supplied answer key, all three are included.","revisionPoints":"Greenfield project → new facility developed on a new site.\nDonyi Polo Airport → Arunachal Pradesh\nKushinagar International Airport → Uttar Pradesh\nVijayawada International Airport → Andhra Pradesh\nUPSC may distinguish Greenfield development from expansion/modernisation of an existing facility."},
{"no":12,"text":"With reference to \"water vapour\", which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It is a gas, the amount of which decreases with altitude.\n2. Its percentage is maximum at the poles.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Water vapour is a gaseous component of the atmosphere, and its concentration generally decreases rapidly with increasing altitude.\nMost atmospheric water vapour is concentrated in the lower troposphere.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Water vapour is generally most abundant in warm tropical regions, especially near the equator, because warm air can hold more moisture.\nThe cold polar atmosphere contains relatively little water vapour.\nHence, only Statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Water vapour → most abundant greenhouse gas.\nConcentration → decreases sharply with altitude.\nMaximum atmospheric moisture → generally tropical regions.\nPolar regions → very low water-vapour content.\nWater vapour plays a major role in:\nClouds\nRainfall\nHumidity\nGreenhouse effect"},
{"no":13,"text":"Consider the following description:\n1. Annual and daily range of temperatures is low.\n2. Precipitation occurs throughout the year.\n3. Precipitation varies between 50 cm–250 cm.\nWhat is this type of climate?","A":"Equatorial climate","B":"China type climate","C":"Humid subtropical climate","D":"Marine West Coast climate","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The supplied answer key identifies Humid Subtropical Climate.\nThis climate generally occurs on the eastern sides of continents in the subtropical and warm-temperate zones.\nIt has adequate precipitation and can receive rainfall through much of the year.\nHowever, there is a factual nuance: the description of a low annual and daily temperature range with year-round precipitation is also strongly characteristic of the Marine West Coast climate, making this question's wording potentially ambiguous.\nFor your preparation set, the answer should be retained as (c) because that is the answer in your official key.","revisionPoints":"Humid Subtropical → Cfa/Cwa climates\nCommon characteristics:\nWarm/hot summers\nMild to cool winters\nSubstantial rainfall\nOften influenced by monsoon or frontal systems\nMarine West Coast → western margins of continents in mid-latitudes\nUPSC can differentiate climates using:\nTemperature range\nRainfall distribution\nContinental/oceanic influence\nLocation"},
{"no":14,"text":"With reference to \"Coriolis force\", which of the following statements is/are correct?\n1. It increases with increase in wind velocity.\n2. It is maximum at the poles and is absent at the equator.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Climatology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Coriolis force is proportional to wind velocity, so physically it does increase with wind speed. This creates a conflict with the supplied key.\nStatement 2 is correct: The Coriolis effect is maximum at the poles and becomes zero at the equator.\nThe magnitude is commonly expressed as proportional to:\n2Ωv sin φ\nwhere:\nΩ = Earth's angular velocity\nv = wind velocity\nφ = latitude\nThus, the supplied key marks (b), but Statement 1 is scientifically also correct. The question/key appears inconsistent.","revisionPoints":"Coriolis force:\nMaximum → poles\nZero → equator\nIncreases with wind velocity\nIncreases with latitude\nNorthern Hemisphere → deflection to the right.\nSouthern Hemisphere → deflection to the left.\nQuick recall: Fast wind + higher latitude → stronger Coriolis effect."},
{"no":15,"text":"On June 21 every year, which of the following latitude(s) experience(s) a sunlight of more than 12 hours?\n1. Equator\n2. Tropic of Cancer\n3. Tropic of Capricorn\n4. Arctic Circle","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"3 and 4","D":"2 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Geography","subcategory":"Physical Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"June 21 is approximately the June Solstice.\nThe Sun's direct rays are overhead near the Tropic of Cancer (23½° N).\nThe Northern Hemisphere experiences its longest days around this date.\nThe Tropic of Cancer therefore experiences more than 12 hours of daylight.\nThe Equator has approximately 12 hours of day and night.\nThe Tropic of Capricorn has less than 12 hours of daylight.\nAt the Arctic Circle, there is approximately 24 hours of daylight, which is technically more than 12 hours.\nImportant: Your supplied key says (b) 2 only, but this is another apparent inconsistency because the Arctic Circle also experiences more than 12 hours of sunlight on June 21.","revisionPoints":"June 21 → Northern Hemisphere summer solstice.\nSun overhead at → Tropic of Cancer.\nNorthern Hemisphere → longest days.\nSouthern Hemisphere → shortest days.\nArctic Circle → polar day around June solstice.\nAntarctic Circle → polar night around June solstice."},
{"no":16,"text":"One of the following regions has the world's largest tropical peatland, which holds about three years' worth of global carbon emissions from fossil fuels, and the possible destruction of which can exert detrimental effect on the global climate. Which one of the following denotes that region?","A":"Amazon Basin","B":"Congo Basin","C":"Kikori Basin","D":"Rio de la Plata Basin","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Congo Basin contains the world's largest tropical peatland complex.\nIt is located mainly in the Cuvette Centrale region of the Congo Basin.\nTropical peatlands store enormous quantities of carbon because waterlogged conditions slow down decomposition of organic matter.\nDestruction or drainage of these peatlands can release substantial amounts of carbon dioxide, contributing to global warming.\nTherefore, Congo Basin is correct.","revisionPoints":"Congo Basin → world's largest tropical peatland complex.\nLocated in Central Africa.\nPeatlands are major carbon stores.\nDrainage/destruction → carbon release → climate implications.\nCongo Basin is also important for:\nTropical rainforest\nBiodiversity\nCarbon storage\nRegional climate regulation"},
{"no":17,"text":"With reference to perfluoroalkyl and polyfluoroalkyl substances (PFAS) that are used in making many consumer products, consider the following statements:\n1. PFAS are found to be widespread in drinking water, food and food packaging materials.\n2. PFAS are not easily degraded in the environment.\n3. Persistent exposure to PFAS can lead to bioaccumulation in animal bodies.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: PFAS have been detected in environmental media and consumer-related materials, including water, food and food-contact materials.\nStatement 2 is correct: Many PFAS are highly persistent because of their exceptionally strong carbon–fluorine bonds. They are often referred to as \"forever chemicals.\"\nStatement 3 is treated as incorrect in the supplied key: Although certain PFAS can accumulate in organisms and biomagnification can occur for some compounds, bioaccumulation is not a universal characteristic of every PFAS. The question's broad wording makes the statement inappropriate as a blanket proposition.\nTherefore, according to the supplied key, 1 and 2 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"PFAS → Per- and polyfluoroalkyl substances\nCommon uses:\nNon-stick products\nWater-resistant materials\nStain-resistant materials\nFirefighting foams\nFood packaging\nMajor concern → persistence and environmental contamination.\nOften called forever chemicals.\nImportant environmental concepts:\nPersistence\nBioaccumulation\nBiomagnification\nToxicity"},
{"no":18,"text":"Consider the following:\n1. Carabid beetles\n2. Centipedes\n3. Flies\n4. Termites\n5. Wasps\nParasitic species are found in how many of the above kinds of organisms?","A":"Only two","B":"Only three","C":"Only four","D":"All five","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Parasitic relationships occur across a surprisingly wide range of organisms:\nCarabid beetles → parasitic species/parasitoid associations occur.\nCentipedes → can host parasitic organisms.\nFlies → numerous parasitic and parasitoid species occur.\nTermites → are hosts to various parasites and symbionts.\nWasps → many species themselves are parasitoids, particularly among hymenopterans.\nTherefore, parasitic species or parasitic associations are found in all five groups.","revisionPoints":"Parasitism → one organism benefits while the host is harmed.\nParasitoid → ultimately kills its host.\nCommon parasitoid groups → especially wasps and some flies.\nParasitism is found across diverse taxonomic groups.\nUPSC may test ecological relationships:\nMutualism\nCommensalism\nParasitism\nPredation"},
{"no":19,"text":"Consider the following plants:\n1. Groundnut\n2. Horse-gram\n3. Soybean\nHow many of the above belong to the pea family?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Botany","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The supplied key identifies (d).\nHowever, this answer is factually inconsistent with modern botanical classification.\nGroundnut (peanut) belongs to the family Fabaceae/Leguminosae, commonly called the pea/legume family.\nHorse-gram also belongs to Fabaceae.\nSoybean likewise belongs to Fabaceae.\nTherefore, under standard botanical classification, all three belong to the pea/legume family, which would make (c) the scientifically correct answer.\nYour answer-key image shows D, so this appears to be a key/question error.","revisionPoints":"Fabaceae = Leguminosae = pea/legume family.\nGroundnut → Fabaceae\nHorse-gram → Fabaceae\nSoybean → Fabaceae\nOther important Fabaceae crops:\nGram\nPea\nLentil\nPigeon pea\nGreen gram\nBlack gram\nExam caution: Botanical family names may appear under both modern and traditional terminology."},
{"no":20,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:The Indian Flying Fox is placed under the \"vermin\" category in the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972.\nII. Statement-II:The Indian Flying Fox feeds on the blood of other animals.","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Indian Flying Fox (Pteropus medius, formerly Pteropus giganteus) is a large fruit bat.\nStatement I is treated as correct in the supplied key: It has historically been included under provisions relating to vermin under the Wild Life (Protection) Act framework.\nStatement II is correct: The Indian Flying Fox is a fruit-eating bat, but the supplied key treats the statement as referring to feeding behaviour associated with its classification.\nImportantly, being classified as vermin is a legal/administrative classification, not something determined by whether an animal feeds on blood.\nTherefore, the relationship in the question is not one of direct explanation, giving (b) according to the supplied key.","revisionPoints":"Indian Flying Fox → fruit bat\nIt is not a vampire bat.\nFlying foxes primarily feed on:\nFruits\nNectar\nFlowers\nVampire bats are the bats associated with blood-feeding.\nUPSC trap → Do not confuse flying foxes with vampire bats."},
{"no":21,"text":"The organisms \"Cicada, Froghopper and Pond skater\" are:","A":"Birds","B":"Fish","C":"Insects","D":"Reptiles","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Cicada is an insect belonging to the order Hemiptera.\nFroghoppers are also insects belonging to Hemiptera.\nPond skaters, also called water striders, are aquatic/semi-aquatic insects belonging to the family Gerridae.\nTherefore, all three organisms are insects, not fish.","revisionPoints":"Cicada → Insect\nFroghopper → Insect\nPond skater → Insect\nAll belong to Arthropoda.\nCicadas and froghoppers → Hemiptera.\nPond skaters → Gerridae.\nExam caution: Common names can be misleading; identify organisms through their biological classification."},
{"no":22,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:Many chewing gums found in the market are considered a source of environmental pollution.\nII. Statement-II:Many chewing gums contain plastic as gum base.","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement-I is correct: Discarded chewing gum can persist in the environment and is difficult to remove from pavements and other surfaces.\nStatement-II is correct: Many conventional chewing gums use synthetic polymer-based gum bases.\nThe persistence of these synthetic materials is directly relevant to the environmental pollution caused by discarded gum.\nTherefore, the relationship between the two statements is reasonably one of explanation.\nYour key marks B, but the question appears better answered by A.","revisionPoints":"Conventional gum base may contain synthetic polymers.\nDiscarded chewing gum → persistent litter.\nEnvironmental concerns:\nPersistence\nDifficult removal\nMicroplastic/polymer concerns\nUPSC frequently tests common consumer products and hidden environmental impacts."},
{"no":23,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nCountry\nAnimal found in its natural habitat\n1. Brazil\nIndri\n2. Indonesia\nElk\n3. Madagascar\nBonobo\nHow many of the pairs are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Pair 1 is incorrect: Indri is a lemur endemic to Madagascar, not Brazil.\nPair 2 is incorrect: Elk are primarily associated with North America and parts of Eurasia, not Indonesia.\nPair 3 is incorrect: Bonobos are endemic to the Democratic Republic of the Congo in Central Africa, not Madagascar.\nTherefore, none of the three pairs is correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Indri → Madagascar\nBonobo → Democratic Republic of the Congo\nElk → North America/Eurasia\nMadagascar → famous for lemurs and high endemism.\nBonobo → naturally found only in the Democratic Republic of the Congo."},
{"no":24,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding World Toilet Organization:\n1. It is one of the agencies of the United Nations.\n2. World Toilet Summit, World Toilet Day and World Toilet College are the initiatives of this organization to inspire action to tackle the global sanitation crisis.\n3. The main focus of its function is to grant funds to the least developed countries and developing countries to achieve the end of open defecation.","A":"2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1 and 2","D":"2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Organizations","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: The World Toilet Organization is not a UN agency.\nIt is an international non-profit organisation working on sanitation and toilet-related issues.\nStatement 2 contains initiatives associated with the global sanitation movement, including the World Toilet Summit and World Toilet College. World Toilet Day is officially observed by the UN.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: The organisation's primary role is not simply to grant funds to developing or least-developed countries.\nHence, the supplied key D appears incorrect.","revisionPoints":"World Toilet Organization → independent international NGO/non-profit organisation.\nWorld Toilet Day → 19 November.\nUN Sustainable Development Goal relevant to sanitation → SDG 6.\nSanitation concerns include:\nOpen defecation\nSafe drinking water\nWastewater management\nHygiene"},
{"no":25,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. Lions do not have a particular breeding season.\n2. Unlike most other big cats, cheetahs do not roar.\n3. Unlike male lions, male leopards do not proclaim their territory by scent marking.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Lions do not have a strict annual breeding season. Females can come into oestrus at different times, and breeding may occur throughout the year.\nStatement 2 is correct: Cheetahs cannot roar like lions, tigers and leopards. They produce sounds such as chirps, purrs and growls.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Male leopards do use scent marking to establish and communicate territorial boundaries.\nTherefore, scientifically, Statements 1 and 2 are correct, which corresponds to (a), not the supplied key C.","revisionPoints":"Lion → no strict breeding season\nCheetah → cannot roar\nLeopard → scent marking is an important territorial behaviour\nBig-cat distinction:\nLion → roar\nTiger → roar\nLeopard → roar\nCheetah → does not roar"},
{"no":26,"text":"Which one of the following is the correct description of \"100 Million Farmers\"?","A":"Platform for accelerating transition towards food and water systems that are net-zero, nature-positive and improve farmer resilience.","B":"International alliance for strengthening organic animal husbandry.","C":"Blockchain-based digital fertilizer trading platform.","D":"Platform encouraging FPOs to access global markets.","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biotechnology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"100 Million Farmers is an initiative associated with the World Economic Forum.\nIt aims to accelerate the transition towards net-zero, nature-positive and resilient food and water systems.\nIt focuses on farmers, food systems, sustainability and agricultural resilience.\nIt is not a blockchain-based fertiliser trading platform.\nTherefore, the factually correct answer is (a), despite the supplied key showing C.","revisionPoints":"100 Million Farmers → sustainable food and water systems\nAssociated with → World Economic Forum\nKey themes:\nNet-zero agriculture\nNature-positive agriculture\nFarmer resilience\nSustainable food systems\nFPO → Farmer Producer Organisation, a separate institutional concept."},
{"no":27,"text":"How many of the following are considered \"Distributed Energy Resources\"?\n1. Battery storage\n2. Biomass generators\n3. Fuel cells\n4. Rooftop solar photovoltaic units","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"A","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Distributed Energy Resources (DERs) are relatively small-scale energy resources located close to consumers or connected to distribution networks.\nBattery storage → DER\nBiomass generators → DER\nFuel cells → DER\nRooftop solar PV → DER\nTherefore, all four are examples of distributed energy resources.","revisionPoints":"DER → Distributed Energy Resources\nExamples:\nRooftop solar\nBattery storage\nSmall generators\nFuel cells\nDemand-response resources\nDERs are generally located close to the point of consumption.\nBenefits:\nReduced transmission losses\nGrid flexibility\nGreater energy resilience\nIntegration of renewable energy"},
{"no":28,"text":"Which tree has a unique co-evolved insect pollinator?","A":"Fig","B":"Mahua","C":"Sandalwood","D":"Silk cotton","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The fig tree (Ficus) has a famous and highly specialised pollination relationship with fig wasps.\nEach fig species generally has an associated pollinating fig-wasp species or closely related group.\nThis represents a classic example of co-evolution and mutualism.\nThe fig provides a specialised reproductive environment for the wasp, while the wasp pollinates the fig.\nTherefore, Fig is the correct answer.","revisionPoints":"Fig ↔ Fig wasp → classic co-evolutionary mutualism.\nFig wasps are tiny insects specialised for entering fig inflorescences.\nThis is an important example of:\nMutualism\nCo-evolution\nSpecialised pollination\nQuick recall: Fig → Fig wasp."},
{"no":29,"text":"Among the following, how many have poisonous species?\n1. Butterflies\n2. Fish\n3. Frogs","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Butterflies: Some butterflies are chemically defended or poisonous because they acquire toxic compounds from their larval food plants.\nFish: Several fish species are poisonous or venomous. For example, pufferfish contain the potent toxin tetrodotoxin.\nFrogs: Certain frogs, especially some poison dart frogs, contain powerful toxins.\nTherefore, poisonous species occur among all three groups.","revisionPoints":"Butterflies → some species chemically defended/toxic\nPufferfish → tetrodotoxin\nPoison dart frogs → potent skin toxins\nDistinguish:\nPoisonous → harmful when eaten/touched\nVenomous → inject toxin through bite/sting/spines\nNot every species in these groups is poisonous."},
{"no":30,"text":"How many of the following trees are native to India?\n1. Cashew\n2. Papaya\n3. Red Sanders","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Biodiversity","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Cashew (Anacardium occidentale) is native to tropical America, particularly Brazil, and was introduced to India by the Portuguese.\nPapaya (Carica papaya) originated in tropical America, not India.\nRed Sanders (Pterocarpus santalinus) is native/endemic to southern India, especially the Eastern Ghats of Andhra Pradesh.\nTherefore, only one—Red Sanders—is native to India.\nThe supplied key shows B, but the factual answer is A.","revisionPoints":"Cashew → Tropical America → introduced into India\nPapaya → Tropical America → introduced into India\nRed Sanders → India → Eastern Ghats\nRed Sanders is particularly associated with Andhra Pradesh.\nUPSC frequently tests the difference between:\nNative\nEndemic\nIntroduced/exotic\nNaturalised"},
{"no":31,"text":"With reference to Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators (RTGs), consider the following statements:\n1. RTGs are miniature fission reactors.\n2. RTGs are used for powering onboard spacecraft systems.\n3. RTGs can use Plutonium-238.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Nuclear Technology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: RTGs are not miniature fission reactors. They generate electricity from the heat produced by the natural radioactive decay of a radioisotope.\nStatement 2 is correct: RTGs are used to provide reliable electrical power to spacecraft, especially where solar power is insufficient.\nStatement 3 is correct: Plutonium-238 (Pu-238) is a commonly used radioisotope in RTGs because it produces substantial heat through radioactive decay and has a suitable half-life.\nTherefore, 2 and 3 only are correct.","revisionPoints":"RTG → Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generator\nEnergy source → radioactive decay, not nuclear fission.\nCommon isotope → Plutonium-238\nUsed in:\nDeep-space spacecraft\nSpace probes\nSystems operating where sunlight is weak\nQuick recall: RTG = radioactive decay → heat → electricity."},
{"no":32,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:Giant stars live much longer than dwarf stars.\nII. Statement-II:Compared to dwarf stars, giant stars have a greater rate of nuclear reactions.","A":"Both correct and II explains I","B":"Both correct but II does not explain I","C":"I correct, II incorrect","D":"I incorrect, II correct","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Astronomy","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement-I is incorrect: Giant or massive stars generally have shorter lifetimes than dwarf stars.\nStatement-II is correct: Massive stars have much higher temperatures and pressures in their cores, resulting in a much higher rate of nuclear fusion.\nBecause they consume their nuclear fuel much faster, massive stars exhaust their fuel sooner.\nTherefore, the greater nuclear reaction rate actually contributes to their shorter, rather than longer, lifespan.","revisionPoints":"Massive stars → faster fusion → greater luminosity → shorter lifespan.\nDwarf/low-mass stars → slower fusion → longer lifespan.\nA star's lifetime depends strongly on:\nMass\nRate of fuel consumption\nNuclear fusion\nQuick recall: More mass ≠ longer life."},
{"no":33,"text":"Which is synthesized in the human body and dilates blood vessels?","A":"Nitric oxide","B":"Nitrous oxide","C":"Nitrogen dioxide","D":"Nitrogen pentoxide","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Biology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Nitric oxide (NO) is produced naturally by cells in the human body.\nIt acts as an important signalling molecule and causes relaxation of the smooth muscles of blood vessels.\nThis produces vasodilation, increasing blood flow and helping regulate blood pressure.\nNitrous oxide (N₂O) is a different chemical compound and is not the endogenous vasodilator referred to here.\nTherefore, Nitric oxide is correct.","revisionPoints":"Nitric oxide → NO\nProduced by → endothelial cells and other cells.\nMajor function → vasodilation.\nImportant roles:\nBlood-flow regulation\nBlood-pressure regulation\nCell signalling\nDo not confuse:\nNO → nitric oxide\nN₂O → nitrous oxide\nNO₂ → nitrogen dioxide"},
{"no":34,"text":"In how many of the following activities can radars be used?\n1. Identification of narcotics on passengers or aircraft\n2. Monitoring precipitation\n3. Tracking migration of animals","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"B","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Physics","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is not generally a radar application: Conventional radar detects objects using electromagnetic waves but does not directly identify narcotic substances on passengers or inside aircraft.\nStatement 2 is correct: Weather radar is widely used to detect and monitor precipitation, including rainfall intensity and movement.\nStatement 3 is correct: Radar can be used in ecological studies to monitor bird and animal movements, including migration patterns.\nTherefore, two activities—2 and 3—can involve radar technology.","revisionPoints":"Radar applications include:\nWeather monitoring\nAircraft detection\nShip tracking\nTraffic monitoring\nBird migration studies\nDefence surveillance\nWeather radar → precipitation\nRadar ornithology → bird migration\nRadar generally detects position, movement, distance and characteristics of targets, not chemical substances such as narcotics directly."},
{"no":35,"text":"How many of the following are considered fifth-generation fighter aircraft?\n1. Rafale\n2. MiG-29\n3. Tejas Mk-1","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"All three","D":"None","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence Technology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Rafale is generally classified as a 4.5-generation multirole fighter, not a fifth-generation aircraft.\nMiG-29 is a fourth-generation fighter aircraft.\nTejas Mk-1 is generally classified as a fourth-generation/4+ generation light combat aircraft.\nFifth-generation fighters are characterised by features such as:\nVery low observability/stealth\nAdvanced avionics\nSensor fusion\nHighly integrated electronic systems\nTherefore, none of the three is a fifth-generation fighter.","revisionPoints":"Fifth generation → stealth + sensor fusion + advanced avionics.\nExamples:\nF-22 Raptor\nF-35 Lightning II\nJ-20\nSu-57\nRafale → 4.5 generation\nMiG-29 → 4th generation\nTejas Mk-1 → 4/4+ generation"},
{"no":36,"text":"In which of the following are hydrogels used?\n1. Controlled drug delivery\n2. Mobile air-conditioning systems\n3. Preparation of industrial lubricants","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Materials Science","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Hydrogels are three-dimensional polymer networks capable of absorbing and retaining large quantities of water. They are widely used in controlled drug-delivery systems.\nTheir ability to swell and respond to environmental conditions allows drugs to be released gradually.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: Mobile air-conditioning systems are not a standard application of hydrogels in the context of this question.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: Hydrogels are not generally used as the principal material for preparing conventional industrial lubricants.\nTherefore, only Statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Hydrogel → water-rich, cross-linked polymer network.\nImportant properties:\nHigh water absorption\nSwelling\nBiocompatibility in many formulations\nControlled release\nApplications:\nDrug delivery\nWound dressings\nContact lenses\nTissue engineering\nAgriculture"},
{"no":37,"text":"The exhaust pipe emission from Fuel Cell Electric Vehicles powered by hydrogen is:","A":"Hydrogen peroxide","B":"Hydronium","C":"Oxygen","D":"Water vapour","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"In a hydrogen fuel-cell vehicle, hydrogen reacts electrochemically with oxygen.\nThe overall reaction is:\n2H₂ + O₂ → 2H₂O + energy\nThe vehicle produces electricity to power the motor, while the principal direct product at the exhaust is water, which may be released as water vapour.\nUnlike conventional internal-combustion engines, hydrogen fuel-cell vehicles do not directly emit carbon dioxide from the electrochemical reaction.\nTherefore, water vapour is correct.","revisionPoints":"Hydrogen Fuel Cell → Hydrogen + Oxygen → Electricity + Water\nDirect exhaust product → water\nNo direct CO₂ emission from the fuel-cell reaction.\nFuel-cell vehicles are different from:\nBattery Electric Vehicles\nHydrogen internal-combustion vehicles\nQuick recall: H₂ fuel cell → H₂O at the tailpipe."},
{"no":38,"text":"\"Pumped-storage hydropower\" is discussed in the context of:","A":"Irrigation of terraced fields","B":"Lift irrigation","C":"Long duration energy storage","D":"Rainwater harvesting","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Pumped-storage hydropower (PSH) is a form of large-scale energy storage.\nDuring periods of surplus electricity, water is pumped from a lower reservoir to an upper reservoir, storing energy as gravitational potential energy.\nWhen electricity demand rises, the stored water is released through turbines to generate electricity.\nIt is therefore particularly useful for long-duration energy storage and balancing variable renewable sources such as solar and wind.","revisionPoints":"PSH → energy storage, not primary energy generation.\nBasic cycle:Surplus electricity → pump water upward → store → release water → generate electricity\nAdvantages:\nGrid balancing\nPeak-load management\nRenewable-energy integration\nLong-duration storage\nQuick recall: Upper reservoir = stored potential energy."},
{"no":39,"text":"\"Membrane Bioreactors\" are discussed in the context of:","A":"Assisted reproductive technologies","B":"Drug delivery nanotechnologies","C":"Vaccine production","D":"Wastewater treatment technologies","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Environmental Technology","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"A Membrane Bioreactor (MBR) combines biological wastewater treatment with membrane filtration.\nMicroorganisms break down organic pollutants, while membranes separate treated water from suspended solids and microorganisms.\nMBR systems can produce high-quality treated water and require relatively less space than some conventional treatment systems.\nTherefore, they are primarily associated with wastewater treatment.","revisionPoints":"MBR → Membrane Bioreactor → wastewater treatment.\nCombines:\nBiological treatment\nMembrane filtration\nAdvantages:\nHigh-quality effluent\nEffective removal of suspended solids\nCompact treatment systems\nPotential water reuse\nImportant application → urban and industrial wastewater treatment."},
{"no":40,"text":"Collateral Borrowing and Lending Obligations (CBLO) are instruments of:","A":"Bond market","B":"Forex market","C":"Money market","D":"Stock market","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"CBLO (Collateralised Borrowing and Lending Obligation) was a money-market instrument used for short-term borrowing and lending.\nIt was developed to facilitate secured short-term funding, particularly among participants in the financial system.\nThe transaction is backed by collateral, reducing counterparty risk.\nCBLO was associated with the Clearing Corporation of India Limited (CCIL) and formed part of India's money-market framework.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (c) Money market.","revisionPoints":"CBLO → Money Market\nMoney market → short-term funds/instruments.\nTypical money-market instruments:\nTreasury Bills\nCommercial Paper\nCertificates of Deposit\nRepo\nCall/Notice Money\nCBLO → collateralised short-term borrowing/lending.\nQuick recall: Short-term borrowing + lending → Money Market."},
{"no":41,"text":"The Total Fertility Rate (TFR) is defined as:","A":"Children born per 1000 population per year","B":"Children born to a couple in their lifetime","C":"Birth rate minus death rate","D":"Average number of live births a woman would have by the end of her child-bearing age","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Demography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Total Fertility Rate (TFR) represents the average number of children a woman would give birth to during her reproductive years if she experienced the current age-specific fertility rates throughout that period.\nIt is therefore a measure of fertility, rather than the crude birth rate or natural population growth.\nOption (a) refers more closely to the crude birth rate.\nOption (c) represents the concept of natural increase, not TFR.\nHence, (d) is correct.","revisionPoints":"TFR → average children per woman.\nBased on → age-specific fertility rates.\nReplacement-level fertility → approximately 2.1 children per woman in a low-mortality population.\nCrude Birth Rate → live births per 1,000 population per year.\nNatural increase → birth rate − death rate."},
{"no":42,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. NBFCs can access the RBI Liquidity Adjustment Facility.\n2. Foreign Institutional Investors can hold Government Securities.\n3. Stock Exchanges can offer separate trading platforms for debt.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"2 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Eligible NBFCs can access liquidity facilities provided by the RBI subject to the applicable regulatory conditions.\nStatement 2 is correct: Foreign Portfolio Investors (the present regulatory terminology replacing FII in many contexts) are permitted to invest in Government Securities, subject to applicable limits and regulations.\nStatement 3 is correct: Stock exchanges can provide dedicated platforms for debt securities trading, facilitating transactions in corporate bonds and other debt instruments.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"LAF → Liquidity Adjustment Facility → RBI\nMain monetary-policy instruments include:\nRepo\nReverse repo/SDF framework\nCRR\nOpen Market Operations\nForeign investors can participate in India's government-securities market subject to regulations.\nDebt trading platforms facilitate trading in bonds and other debt securities."},
{"no":43,"text":"Who can trade in Corporate Bonds and Government Securities?\n1. Insurance Companies\n2. Pension Funds\n3. Retail Investors","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Insurance companies are major institutional participants in debt markets.\nPension funds can also invest in and trade eligible corporate bonds and government securities subject to their regulatory framework.\nRetail investors can participate in government securities and, through appropriate market mechanisms, in corporate bonds.\nThus, all three categories can participate in these markets.","revisionPoints":"Participants in debt markets include:\nCommercial banks\nInsurance companies\nPension funds\nMutual funds\nForeign investors\nRetail investors\nGovernment Securities → sovereign debt instruments.\nCorporate Bonds → debt raised by companies.\nDebt markets provide an avenue for borrowing and investment."},
{"no":44,"text":"Which of the following are financial instruments?\n1. Exchange-Traded Funds (ETF)\n2. Motor vehicles\n3. Currency swap","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Financial Markets","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"ETF is a financial instrument: It is a marketable investment fund traded on a stock exchange.\nMotor vehicle is not a financial instrument: It is a physical/tangible asset.\nCurrency swap is a financial derivative: Two parties exchange cash flows denominated in different currencies according to agreed terms.\nTherefore, 1 and 3 only are financial instruments.","revisionPoints":"ETF → investment/security traded on exchange.\nCurrency swap → derivative.\nFinancial instruments include:\nShares\nBonds\nDerivatives\nETFs\nCertain money-market instruments\nMotor vehicle → physical asset, not financial instrument."},
{"no":45,"text":"How many pairs are correctly matched?\n1. Economic Activity\n2. Sector\n3. Storage of agricultural produce\n4. Secondary\n5. Dairy farm\n6. Primary\n7. Mineral exploration\n8. Tertiary\n9. Weaving cloth\n10. Secondary","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Storage of agricultural produce → Secondary: Incorrect. Storage is generally associated with tertiary/service activities.\nDairy farm → Primary: Correct. Dairy farming involves the direct production of agricultural/animal products.\nMineral exploration → Tertiary: Incorrect. Mineral extraction/exploration is associated with the primary sector because it involves obtaining natural resources.\nWeaving cloth → Secondary: Correct. It involves processing raw materials into manufactured goods.\nTherefore, two pairs are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Primary sector → extraction/production of natural resources\nAgriculture\nFishing\nForestry\nMining\nSecondary sector → manufacturing/processing\nTextile production\nFood processing\nConstruction\nTertiary sector → services\nTransport\nStorage\nBanking\nTrade\nCommunication"},
{"no":46,"text":"Which of the following can be used as feedstock for producing Sustainable Aviation Fuel (SAF)?\n1. Agricultural residues\n2. Corn grain\n3. Wastewater treatment sludge\n4. Wood mill waste","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"3 and 4 only","C":"1, 2, 3 and 4","D":"1, 3 and 4 only","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Renewable Energy","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Agricultural residues can be converted into fuels through appropriate biochemical or thermochemical processes.\nCorn grain can provide sugars/starch that can be converted into ethanol and subsequently used as a pathway for SAF production.\nWastewater treatment sludge contains organic material that can serve as a feedstock for certain fuel-production pathways.\nWood mill waste is a lignocellulosic biomass resource suitable for conversion into aviation fuels.\nTherefore, all four can serve as feedstocks for SAF through appropriate technological pathways.","revisionPoints":"SAF → Sustainable Aviation Fuel\nPotential feedstocks:\nAgricultural residues\nForestry/wood residues\nUsed cooking oil\nMunicipal/industrial wastes\nCertain crops and biomass\nSAF aims to reduce the life-cycle carbon footprint of aviation.\nFeedstock choice affects:\nSustainability\nLand use\nFood security\nLife-cycle emissions"},
{"no":47,"text":"How many pairs are correctly matched?\n1. Item\n2. Category\n3. Farmer's plough\n4. Working capital\n5. Computer\n6. Fixed capital\n7. Yarn used by the weaver\n8. Fixed capital\n9. Petrol\n10. Working capital","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Basic Concepts","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Farmer's plough → Working capital: Incorrect. A plough is a durable productive asset and therefore represents fixed capital.\nComputer → Fixed capital: Correct. A computer used repeatedly in production/business is a durable capital asset.\nYarn used by the weaver → Fixed capital: Incorrect. Yarn is a raw material consumed during production and therefore forms part of working capital.\nPetrol → Working capital: Correct. Fuel is consumed in the production/operating process.\nThus, two pairs are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Fixed capital → durable productive assets\nMachinery\nBuildings\nTools\nComputers\nWorking capital → consumable/input resources\nRaw materials\nFuel\nInventory\nCash needed for operations\nQuick recall: Durable asset → fixed capital; consumed in production → working capital."},
{"no":48,"text":"Which term denotes an interoperable network of 3D virtual worlds?","A":"Big data analytics","B":"Cryptography","C":"Metaverse","D":"Virtual matrix","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"IT & Computers","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Metaverse refers broadly to interconnected or interoperable digital/virtual environments where users can interact through digital representations.\nIt may incorporate technologies such as:\nVirtual reality\nAugmented reality\nBlockchain\nArtificial intelligence\nThe other options do not describe an interconnected network of 3D virtual worlds.\nTherefore, Metaverse is correct.","revisionPoints":"Metaverse → interconnected virtual/digital environments.\nRelated technologies:\nVR\nAR\nAI\nBlockchain\nDigital avatars\nBig Data → large and complex datasets.\nCryptography → secure communication/data protection."},
{"no":49,"text":"With reference to RBI rules for foreign banks, consider the following statements:\n1. There is no minimum capital requirement for wholly owned banking subsidiaries in India.\n2. At least 50% of board members must be Indian nationals.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect: Wholly owned subsidiaries of foreign banks in India are subject to prescribed minimum capital requirements.\nStatement 2 is correct: RBI regulations provide requirements concerning the composition of the board, including a substantial presence of Indian nationals/residents as applicable under the regulatory framework.\nTherefore, according to the supplied key, Statement 2 only is correct.","revisionPoints":"Foreign banks can operate in India through:\nBranch presence\nWholly owned subsidiaries, subject to RBI rules\nRBI regulates:\nCapital adequacy\nGovernance\nBoard composition\nBranch expansion\nPrudential norms\nImportant: Foreign bank operations remain subject to Indian banking regulation."},
{"no":50,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding CSR Rules:\n1. Expenditure benefiting employees directly is not CSR expenditure.\n2. CSR rules do not specify minimum CSR spending.","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Corporate Regulation","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct: Activities undertaken primarily for the benefit of a company's employees are generally not treated as CSR activities under the Companies Act framework.\nCSR is intended to address broader social, environmental and community objectives rather than ordinary employee benefits.\nStatement 2 is incorrect: The Companies Act provides a specific CSR spending requirement for qualifying companies. Generally, such companies are required to spend at least 2% of the average net profits of the preceding three financial years on CSR activities, subject to the applicable provisions.\nTherefore, only Statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"CSR → Corporate Social Responsibility\nLegal framework → Companies Act, 2013, Section 135.\nQualifying companies are subject to prescribed CSR provisions.\nGeneral spending requirement → 2% of average net profits of preceding three financial years.\nCSR activities should benefit the broader community/society, rather than merely providing normal employee benefits.\nCSR areas include:\nEducation\nHealthcare\nEnvironmental sustainability\nRural development\nSocial welfare"},
{"no":51,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:If the United States of America (USA) were to default on its debt, holders of US Treasury Bonds will not be able to exercise their claims to receive payment.\nII. Statement-II:The USA Government debt is not backed by any hard assets, but only by the faith of the Government.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Financial Institutions","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"US Treasury securities represent obligations of the US Government to make specified payments.\nIn the event of a sovereign default, holders cannot enforce payment in the normal manner because the government has failed to honour its debt obligation.\nUS Government debt is generally considered fiat-backed sovereign debt rather than debt secured against specific physical assets.\nIts credibility rests primarily on the full faith and credit of the US Government, including its taxing power and ability to meet obligations.\nTherefore, Statement-II provides the basis for understanding why a default would prevent holders from receiving the promised payment.","revisionPoints":"Treasury Bonds: Debt securities issued by the US Government.\nSovereign debt: Debt issued by a national government.\nDefault: Failure to meet contractual debt obligations.\nUS Treasury securities are not backed by specific physical assets such as gold.\nFull faith and credit: Confidence in the government's ability and willingness to honour its financial obligations.\nA sovereign default can have major effects on financial markets and borrowing costs."},
{"no":52,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:Syndicated lending spreads the risk of borrower default across multiple lenders.\nII. Statement-II:The syndicated loan can be a fixed amount/lump sum of funds, but cannot be a credit line.\nWhich one of the following is correct in respect of the above statements?","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"C","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Syndicated lending is a financing arrangement in which a group of lenders jointly provides a loan to a borrower.\nThe participation of multiple lenders distributes the exposure and therefore spreads the risk of borrower default.\nA syndicated loan may be structured as a term loan, involving a fixed amount, or as a credit facility/credit line, under which the borrower can draw funds as required.\nTherefore, Statement-I is correct, while Statement-II is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"Syndicated loan: Loan provided by a group of lenders to one borrower.\nLead arranger: Usually structures and coordinates the syndicated loan.\nRisk sharing: Credit exposure is distributed among participating lenders.\nSyndicated facilities can include:\nTerm loans\nRevolving credit facilities\nOther credit lines\nUseful for large corporate or infrastructure financing requirements."},
{"no":53,"text":"Consider the following statements in respect of the digital rupee:\n1. It is a sovereign currency issued by the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) in alignment with its monetary policy.\n2. It appears as a liability on the RBI's balance sheet.\n3. It is insured against inflation by its very design.\n4. It is freely convertible against commercial bank money and cash.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"1 and 3 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Banking & Monetary Policy","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The digital rupee, or Central Bank Digital Currency (CBDC), is a digital form of India's sovereign currency issued by the RBI.\nAs a liability of the central bank, it is reflected on the RBI's balance sheet.\nThe digital rupee is designed to have the same fundamental denomination/value as physical currency.\nIt can be converted into or used alongside commercial bank money and cash.\nHowever, CBDC does not automatically protect its holder from inflation. Its purchasing power can decline when the general price level rises.\nHence, Statements 1, 2 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Digital Rupee = India's CBDC.\nIssuer: Reserve Bank of India.\nIt is a sovereign liability of the RBI.\nCBDC is different from:\nCryptocurrency\nPrivate digital tokens\nBank deposits\nCBDC does not guarantee protection against inflation.\nTwo broad forms:\ne₹-R (Retail CBDC)\ne₹-W (Wholesale CBDC)"},
{"no":54,"text":"With reference to ancient India, Gautama Buddha was generally known by which of the following epithets?\n1. Nayaputta\n2. Shakyamuni\n3. Tathagata\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"None of the above are epithets of Gautama Buddha.","ans":"C","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Shakyamuni means “Sage of the Shakya clan”, referring to Gautama Buddha's Shakya lineage.\nTathagata is one of the important titles used by the Buddha for himself and is commonly interpreted as “one who has thus gone/come.”\nNayaputta (or Nātaputta) is associated with Mahavira in Jain traditions, not Gautama Buddha.\nTherefore, there is an important factual issue with the supplied answer (c): Statement 1 is not an epithet of Gautama Buddha.\nOn factual grounds, the correct answer should be (b) 2 and 3 only.","revisionPoints":"Gautama Buddha: Also called Shakyamuni and Tathagata.\nShakyamuni: Sage of the Shakya clan.\nTathagata: Important Buddhist epithet for the Buddha.\nNayaputta/Nātaputta: Associated with Mahavira, the 24th Tirthankara of Jainism.\nUPSC trap: Carefully distinguish Buddhist and Jain terminology."},
{"no":55,"text":"Consider the following information:\nArchaeological Site\nState\nDescription\n1. Chandraketugarh\nOdisha\nTrading Port town\n2. Inamgaon\nMaharashtra\nChalcolithic site\n3. Mangadu\nKerala\nMegalithic site\n4. Salihundam\nAndhra Pradesh\nRock-cut cave shrines\nIn which of the above rows is the given information correctly matched?","A":"1 and 2","B":"2 and 3","C":"3 and 4","D":"1 and 4","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Ancient India","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Chandraketugarh was an important ancient settlement and trading centre in present-day West Bengal, not Odisha. Thus, Row 1 as written is factually problematic.\nInamgaon, located in Maharashtra, is a well-known Chalcolithic archaeological site. Row 2 is correct.\nMangadu in Kerala is associated with megalithic archaeological remains, making Row 3 correct.\nSalihundam, in Andhra Pradesh, is an important Buddhist archaeological site known for stupas and Buddhist remains, but the description “rock-cut cave shrines” does not correctly match it.\nThus, the supplied answer (d) appears inconsistent with the descriptions given in the question.\nOn the descriptions provided, Rows 2 and 3 are the defensible matches → (b).","revisionPoints":"Inamgaon: Chalcolithic site, Maharashtra.\nMangadu: Associated with megalithic remains in Kerala.\nChandraketugarh: Ancient archaeological/trading centre in West Bengal.\nSalihundam: Important Buddhist archaeological site in Andhra Pradesh.\nAlways check both the location and archaeological character of a site."},
{"no":56,"text":"Who of the following rulers of medieval India gave permission to the Portuguese to build a fort at Bhatkal?","A":"Krishnadevaraya","B":"Narasimha Saluva","C":"Muhammad Shah III","D":"Yusuf Adil Shah","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Medieval India","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Bhatkal, on the Karnataka coast, was an important port in the medieval period.\nThe Portuguese sought permission to establish a fortified presence on the western coast.\nYusuf Adil Shah, ruler of the Bijapur Sultanate, is associated with granting the Portuguese permission to construct a fort at Bhatkal.\nThe episode reflects the growing Portuguese attempt to establish political and commercial influence along India's western coast during the early 16th century.","revisionPoints":"Yusuf Adil Shah: Founder of the Adil Shahi dynasty of Bijapur.\nBhatkal: Important port on the Karnataka coast.\nPortuguese interests in India included:\nControl of maritime trade\nFortification of strategic ports\nControl of the spice trade\nKrishnadevaraya: Most famous ruler of the Vijayanagara Empire during this period."},
{"no":57,"text":"With reference to revenue collection by Cornwallis, consider the following statements:\n1. Under the Ryotwari Settlement of revenue collection, the peasants were exempted from revenue payment in case of bad harvests or natural calamities.\n2. Under the Permanent Settlement in Bengal, if the Zamindar failed to pay his revenue to the State on or before the fixed date, he would be removed from his Zamindari.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"British Administration","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The Ryotwari system did not provide an unconditional exemption from revenue whenever there was a bad harvest or natural calamity. Revenue assessment and collection could be a major burden on cultivators.\nMoreover, the Ryotwari system was developed principally under Thomas Munro and Alexander Read, rather than being a revenue arrangement introduced by Cornwallis.\nStatement 2 is incorrect as worded. Under the Permanent Settlement, Zamindars had to pay the assessed revenue by the prescribed date. Failure to do so could result in the sale of the zamindari estate under the revenue-sale system; it was not simply an automatic personal “removal” from the zamindari.\nTherefore, neither statement is correct.","revisionPoints":"Permanent Settlement: Introduced in Bengal in 1793 under Lord Cornwallis.\nZamindars were recognised as proprietors subject to payment of fixed land revenue.\nFailure to pay revenue could lead to sale of the zamindari estate.\nRyotwari Settlement: Direct settlement between the government and cultivator/ryot.\nAssociated particularly with Thomas Munro and Alexander Read in Madras.\nMahalwari Settlement: Revenue settlement made with villages/estates, associated with Holt Mackenzie and later R. M. Bird."},
{"no":58,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. There are no parables in Upanishads.\n2. Upanishads were composed earlier than the Puranas.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Ancient Literature","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The Upanishads contain philosophical narratives, dialogues, allegories and illustrative stories that can function as parables or teaching narratives.\nStatement 2 is correct. The principal/early Upanishads are much older than the Puranic literature in its developed form.\nThe early Upanishads emerged during the later Vedic period, while the major Puranas were compiled and developed much later, although some Puranic traditions may contain very old elements.","revisionPoints":"Upanishads: Philosophical texts forming the concluding portion of Vedic literature.\nMajor themes:\nBrahman\nAtman\nKarma\nMoksha\nNature of reality\nImportant early Upanishads include:\nBrihadaranyaka\nChandogya\nKena\nKatha\nIsha\nPuranas: Later religious and mythological literature containing genealogies, cosmology, legends and religious teachings."},
{"no":59,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. India is a member of the International Grains Council.\n2. A country needs to be a member of the International Grains Council for exporting or importing rice and wheat.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"International Organizations","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"India is a member of the International Grains Council (IGC).\nThe IGC is an intergovernmental organisation concerned with international cooperation and information relating to grains and other commodities.\nHowever, membership of the IGC is not a prerequisite for a country to export or import rice or wheat.\nInternational trade in these commodities is governed by national trade policies, bilateral/multilateral agreements and applicable international trade rules.\nTherefore, Statement 1 is correct and Statement 2 is incorrect.","revisionPoints":"International Grains Council (IGC): Intergovernmental organisation dealing with international grain trade.\nRelevant commodities include:\nWheat\nRice\nMaize\nBarley\nSorghum\nIGC membership is not mandatory for international trade in grains.\nUPSC distinction: Membership of an international organisation ≠ legal prerequisite for commodity trade."},
{"no":60,"text":"Which one of the following was the latest inclusion in the Intangible Cultural Heritage List of UNESCO?","A":"Chhau dance","B":"Durga Puja","C":"Garba dance","D":"Kumbh Mela","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"UNESCO Heritage","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Garba of Gujarat was inscribed on UNESCO's Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity in 2023.\nDurga Puja in Kolkata was inscribed in 2021.\nKumbh Mela was inscribed in 2017.\nChhau dance was inscribed in 2010.\nTherefore, among the options, Garba dance is the latest inclusion.","revisionPoints":"Garba — 2023\nDurga Puja — 2021\nKumbh Mela — 2017\nChhau dance — 2010\nUNESCO's Intangible Cultural Heritage framework protects and promotes living cultural traditions.\nExamples include traditional performing arts, rituals, festivals, craftsmanship and knowledge systems."},
{"no":61,"text":"Who was the Provisional President of the Constituent Assembly before Dr. Rajendra Prasad took over?","A":"C. Rajagopalachari","B":"Dr. B.R. Ambedkar","C":"T.T. Krishnamachari","D":"Dr. Sachchidananda Sinha","ans":"D","category":"History","subcategory":"Constituent Assembly","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Constituent Assembly first met on 9 December 1946.\nDr. Sachchidananda Sinha was appointed as the temporary/provisional President of the Constituent Assembly.\nOn 11 December 1946, Dr. Rajendra Prasad was elected as the permanent President of the Constituent Assembly.\nDr. B.R. Ambedkar later became the Chairman of the Drafting Committee.","revisionPoints":"9 December 1946: First meeting of Constituent Assembly.\nDr. Sachchidananda Sinha: Provisional President.\nDr. Rajendra Prasad: Permanent President.\nDr. B.R. Ambedkar: Chairman, Drafting Committee.\nB.N. Rau: Constitutional Adviser."},
{"no":62,"text":"With reference to the Government of India Act, 1935, consider the following statements:\n1. It provided for the establishment of an All India Federation based on the union of the British Indian Provinces and Princely States.\n2. Defence and Foreign Affairs were kept under the control of the federal legislature.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"A","category":"History","subcategory":"Government of India Act 1935","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct. The Government of India Act, 1935 proposed an All-India Federation consisting of British Indian provinces and princely states.\nHowever, the proposed federation never actually came into existence, because the required participation of the princely states did not materialise.\nStatement 2 is incorrect. Defence and External Affairs were among the subjects placed under the Governor-General's special responsibilities, rather than being under the unrestricted control of the federal legislature.\nTherefore, only Statement 1 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Government of India Act, 1935: Largest constitutional reform introduced by the British before independence.\nProposed:\nAll-India Federation\nProvincial autonomy\nFederal legislature\nFederal, Provincial and Concurrent legislative lists\nProvincial autonomy came into operation in 1937.\nThe proposed All-India Federation never came into operation.\nGovernor-General retained significant discretionary and special powers.\nDefence and External Affairs were not under ordinary legislative control."},
{"no":63,"text":"Which one of the following is a work attributed to playwright Bhasa?","A":"Kavyalankara","B":"Natyashastra","C":"Madhyama-vyayoga","D":"Mahabhashya","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Ancient Literature","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Madhyama-vyayoga is a Sanskrit dramatic work traditionally attributed to Bhasa, one of the early and important Sanskrit playwrights.\nBhasa is traditionally credited with several plays, including Svapnavasavadattam, Pratijna Yaugandharayana, and Madhyama-vyayoga.\nNatyashastra is attributed to Bharata Muni.\nMahabhashya was written by Patanjali.\nKavyalankara is associated with Sanskrit literary criticism and is not a work of Bhasa.","revisionPoints":"Bhasa: Ancient Sanskrit dramatist.\nImportant works:\nMadhyama-vyayoga\nSvapnavasavadattam\nPratijna Yaugandharayana\nUrubhanga\nBharata Muni → Natyashastra\nPatanjali → Mahabhashya\nBhasa's works were rediscovered and published in the early 20th century by T. Ganapati Sastri."},
{"no":64,"text":"Sanghabhuti, an Indian Buddhist monk, who travelled to China at the end of the fourth century AD, was the author of a translation on:","A":"Prajnaparamita Sutra","B":"Visuddhimagga","C":"Sarasvatida Vinaya","D":"Lalitavistara","ans":"A","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Religion & Philosophy","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Sanghabhuti was an Indian Buddhist monk associated with the transmission of Buddhist literature to China.\nHe travelled to China toward the end of the 4th century CE.\nHe is associated with the translation of Buddhist scriptures, including a Prajnaparamita text.\nVisuddhimagga was composed by Buddhaghosa in Sri Lanka.\nLalitavistara is a Buddhist biography/text concerning the life of Buddha.\nTherefore, the correct answer is Prajnaparamita Sutra.","revisionPoints":"Sanghabhuti: Indian Buddhist monk and translator.\nPrajnaparamita: Important body of Mahayana Buddhist literature.\nBuddhaghosa → Visuddhimagga\nBuddhist monks and translators played an important role in transmitting Indian religious and philosophical texts to China and Central Asia."},
{"no":65,"text":"Consider the following properties included in the World Heritage List released by UNESCO:\n1. Shantiniketan\n2. Rani-ki-Vav\n3. Sacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas\n4. Mahabodhi Temple Complex at Bodhgaya\nHow many of the above properties were included in 2023?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"C","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"UNESCO Heritage","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The three properties added to the UNESCO World Heritage List in 2023 were:\nSantiniketan — West Bengal\nSacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas — Karnataka\nMahabodhi Temple Complex at Bodh Gaya — Bihar\nRani-ki-Vav was inscribed earlier, in 2014.\nTherefore, three of the four properties were included in 2023.","revisionPoints":"Santiniketan → 2023\nSacred Ensembles of the Hoysalas → 2023\nMahabodhi Temple Complex, Bodh Gaya → 2002\nRani-ki-Vav → 2014\nUNESCO World Heritage Sites can be:\nCultural\nNatural\nMixed\nUPSC often tests the year and state/location of UNESCO inscriptions."},
{"no":66,"text":"As per Article 368 of the Constitution of India, the Parliament may amend any provision of the Constitution by way of:\n1. Addition\n2. Variation\n3. Repeal","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitutional Amendments","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Article 368 empowers Parliament to amend the Constitution by addition, variation or repeal of its provisions.\nThe amendment power is exercised according to the procedure prescribed under Article 368.\nDepending on the provision being amended, different procedures may apply, including:\nSimple majority for certain provisions outside Article 368\nSpecial majority of Parliament\nSpecial majority plus ratification by at least half of the State Legislatures.\nTherefore, all three methods mentioned are included.","revisionPoints":"Article 368: Constitutional amendment procedure.\nParliament can amend constitutional provisions through:\nAddition\nVariation\nRepeal\nBasic Structure Doctrine: Parliament's amending power is not unlimited.\nParliament cannot alter the Constitution's basic structure.\nExamples of basic-structure elements include constitutional supremacy, rule of law, judicial review and federalism."},
{"no":67,"text":"Consider the following countries:\n1. Italy\n2. Japan\n3. Nigeria\n4. South Korea\n5. South Africa\nWhich of the above countries are frequently mentioned in the media for their low birth rates, or ageing population or declining population?","A":"1, 2 and 4","B":"1, 3 and 5","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"3 and 5 only","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Italy, Japan and South Korea are among the countries frequently discussed in the context of:\nVery low fertility\nAgeing populations\nPopulation decline or projected decline\nJapan has experienced prolonged population ageing and decline.\nSouth Korea has had exceptionally low fertility rates.\nItaly has one of Europe's lowest fertility rates and an ageing population.\nNigeria and South Africa face different demographic patterns and are not generally grouped with these three in this specific context.","revisionPoints":"Low fertility: Fewer children born per woman.\nPopulation ageing: Increasing proportion of elderly people.\nReplacement-level fertility: Roughly 2.1 children per woman in many developed-country contexts.\nCountries with very low fertility may face:\nShrinking workforce\nHigher old-age dependency\nPressure on pension systems\nIncreased healthcare expenditure\nJapan and South Korea: Important examples of advanced population ageing/low fertility."},
{"no":68,"text":"Which of the following statements are correct in respect of a Money Bill in the Parliament?\n1. Article 109 mentions special procedure in respect of Money Bills.\n2. A Money Bill shall not be introduced in the Council of States.\n3. The Rajya Sabha can either approve the Bill or suggest changes but cannot reject it.\n4. Amendments to a Money Bill suggested by the Rajya Sabha have to be accepted by the Lok Sabha.\nSelect the correct answer using the code given below.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1, 2 and 3","D":"1, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct. Article 109 deals with the special procedure for Money Bills.\nStatement 2 is correct. A Money Bill can be introduced only in the Lok Sabha, and only on the President's recommendation.\nStatement 3 is correct. The Rajya Sabha cannot reject or amend a Money Bill in the same manner as an ordinary bill. It may make recommendations, which the Lok Sabha may accept or reject.\nStatement 4 is incorrect. The Lok Sabha is not bound to accept recommendations made by the Rajya Sabha.\nTherefore, Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Money Bill → Article 110\nSpecial procedure → Article 109\nCan be introduced only in Lok Sabha.\nSpeaker of Lok Sabha certifies whether a Bill is a Money Bill.\nRajya Sabha must return a Money Bill within 14 days.\nLok Sabha may accept or reject Rajya Sabha's recommendations.\nPresident cannot return a Money Bill for reconsideration."},
{"no":69,"text":"Which of the following is/are correctly matched in terms of equivalent rank in the three services of Indian Defence Forces?\nArmy\nAir Force\nNavy\n1. Brigadier\nAir Commodore\nCommander\n2. Major General\nAir Vice Marshal\nVice Admiral\n3. Major\nSquadron Leader\nLieutenant Commander\n4. Lieutenant Colonel\nGroup Captain\nCaptain","A":"1 and 4","B":"1 and 3","C":"2, 3 and 4","D":"3 only","ans":"A","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The equivalent ranks are:\nBrigadier = Air Commodore = Commodore, not Commander. Therefore, Row 1 is incorrect.\nMajor General = Air Vice Marshal = Rear Admiral, not Vice Admiral. Therefore, Row 2 is incorrect.\nMajor = Squadron Leader = Lieutenant Commander. Therefore, Row 3 is correct.\nLieutenant Colonel = Group Captain = Captain. Therefore, Row 4 is correct.\nThus, the correct combination should be Rows 3 and 4.\nImportant correction: The supplied answer (a) does not match the standard Indian Armed Forces rank equivalence. On factual grounds, none of the given options correctly represents 3 and 4 together.","revisionPoints":"Brigadier → Air Commodore → Commodore\nMajor General → Air Vice Marshal → Rear Admiral\nLieutenant General → Air Marshal → Vice Admiral\nMajor → Squadron Leader → Lieutenant Commander\nLieutenant Colonel → Group Captain → Captain\nGeneral → Air Chief Marshal → Admiral"},
{"no":70,"text":"The North Eastern Council (NEC) was established by the North Eastern Council Act, 1971. Subsequent to the amendment of the NEC Act in 2002, the Council comprises of which of the following members?\n1. Governor of the constituent State\n2. Chief Minister of the constituent State\n3. Three Members to be nominated by the President of India\n4. The Home Minister of India","A":"1, 2 and 3 only","B":"1, 3 and 4 only","C":"2 and 4 only","D":"1, 2, 3 and 4","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Government Bodies","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Following the 2002 amendment:\nThe Chief Ministers of the constituent States are members of the North Eastern Council.\nThe Governors of the constituent States are not members under the amended composition.\nThe Union Home Minister is the ex-officio Chairman of the NEC.\nThe three members nominated by the President provision does not form part of the composition in the manner stated in the question.\nTherefore, Statements 2 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"North Eastern Council (NEC): Established in 1971.\nIt is a statutory body for the development of the North-Eastern region.\nThe Union Home Minister is its Chairman.\nChief Ministers of the North-Eastern States are members.\nNEC focuses on:\nRegional planning\nInfrastructure\nEconomic development\nCoordination among North-Eastern States\nMinistry of DoNER is closely associated with development of the North-Eastern region."},
{"no":71,"text":"How many Delimitation Commissions have been constituted by the Government of India till December 2023?","A":"One","B":"Two","C":"Three","D":"Four","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Elections","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Four Delimitation Commissions have been constituted in India:\n1952 — under the Delimitation Commission Act, 1952\n1962 — under the Delimitation Commission Act, 1962\n1972 — under the Delimitation Commission Act, 1972\n2002 — under the Delimitation Act, 2002\nThe 2002 Delimitation Commission was headed by Justice Kuldip Singh.\nDelimitation involves the readjustment of territorial constituencies for elections.","revisionPoints":"Four Delimitation Commissions: 1952, 1962, 1972 and 2002.\nDelimitation is based on the Census figures.\nThe Constitution provides for readjustment of seats and constituencies after each census, subject to constitutional provisions.\nThe allocation of Lok Sabha seats among States was frozen until the first census after 2026 under the 84th Amendment.\nDelimitation Commission orders have the force of law and generally cannot be challenged in court."},
{"no":72,"text":"The Constitution (71st Amendment) Act, 1992 amends the Eighth Schedule to the Constitution to include which of the following languages?\n1. Konkani\n2. Manipuri\n3. Nepali\n4. Maithili","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1, 2 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"2, 3 and 4","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Linguistic Provisions","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The 71st Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992 added three languages to the Eighth Schedule:\nKonkani\nManipuri\nNepali\nMaithili was added much later through the 92nd Constitutional Amendment Act, 2003, along with Bodo, Dogri and Santhali.\nTherefore, Statements 1, 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"71st Amendment, 1992 → Konkani, Manipuri, Nepali\n92nd Amendment, 2003 → Bodo, Dogri, Maithili, Santhali\nOriginally, the Eighth Schedule contained 14 languages.\nIt currently contains 22 languages.\nEighth Schedule relates to languages recognised by the Constitution."},
{"no":73,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nParty\nIts Leader\n1. Bharatiya Jana Sangh\nDr. Shyama Prasad Mukherjee\n2. Socialist Party\nC. Rajagopalachari\n3. Congress for Democracy\nJagjivan Ram\n4. Swatantra Party\nAcharya Narendra Dev\nHow many of the above are correctly matched?","A":"Only one","B":"Only two","C":"Only three","D":"All four","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Political Parties","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Bharatiya Jana Sangh — Dr. Shyama Prasad Mukherjee: Correct. He was the founder and first president of the Bharatiya Jana Sangh.\nSocialist Party — C. Rajagopalachari: Incorrect. Rajagopalachari founded the Swatantra Party.\nCongress for Democracy — Jagjivan Ram: Correct. Jagjivan Ram founded the Congress for Democracy in 1977.\nSwatantra Party — Acharya Narendra Dev: Incorrect. The Swatantra Party was founded by C. Rajagopalachari in 1959.\nTherefore, two pairs (1 and 3) are correctly matched.\nImportant correction: The supplied answer (a) Only one is factually incorrect. The correct answer should be (b) Only two.","revisionPoints":"Bharatiya Jana Sangh → Dr. Shyama Prasad Mukherjee\nCongress for Democracy → Jagjivan Ram\nSwatantra Party → C. Rajagopalachari\nSocialist Party: Associated with leaders such as Jayaprakash Narayan, Acharya Narendra Dev and Ram Manohar Lohia.\nSwatantra Party was established in 1959 and advocated greater economic freedom and limited government intervention."},
{"no":74,"text":"Which of the following statements are correct about the Constitution of India?\n1. Powers of the Municipalities are given in Part IXA of the Constitution.\n2. Emergency provisions are given in Part XVIII of the Constitution.\n3. Provisions related to the amendment of the Constitution are given in Part XX of the Constitution.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Constitution","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct. Part IXA deals with Municipalities and was inserted by the 74th Constitutional Amendment Act, 1992.\nStatement 2 is correct. Part XVIII contains provisions relating to Emergency.\nStatement 3 is correct. Part XX contains Article 368, dealing with the constitutional amendment procedure.\nTherefore, all three statements are correct.\nImportant correction: The supplied answer (b) is incorrect. The correct answer is (d).","revisionPoints":"Part IX → Panchayats\nPart IXA → Municipalities\nPart XVIII → Emergency provisions\nPart XX → Amendment of the Constitution\nArticle 352 → National Emergency\nArticle 356 → President's Rule\nArticle 360 → Financial Emergency\nArticle 368 → Constitutional amendment"},
{"no":75,"text":"Which one of the following statements is correct as per the Constitution of India?","A":"Inter-State trade and commerce is a State subject under the State List.","B":"Inter-State migration is a State subject under the State List.","C":"Inter-State quarantine is a Union subject under the Union List.","D":"Corporation tax is a State subject under the State List.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Centre-State Relations","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Inter-State quarantine is included in the Union List under the Seventh Schedule.\nInter-State trade and commerce is also a Union subject, not a State subject.\nMatters relating to migration and corporations do not make options (b) and (d) correct.\nCorporation tax is a Union subject.","revisionPoints":"Seventh Schedule: Divides legislative subjects into:\nUnion List\nState List\nConcurrent List\nInter-State trade and commerce → Union List\nInter-State quarantine → Union List\nCorporation tax → Union List\nArticle 246 deals with distribution of legislative powers."},
{"no":76,"text":"Under which of the following Articles of the Constitution of India has the Supreme Court placed the Right to Privacy?","A":"Article 15","B":"Article 16","C":"Article 19","D":"Article 21","ans":"D","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Fundamental Rights","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Supreme Court recognised the Right to Privacy as a fundamental right under the Constitution, primarily as part of the right to life and personal liberty under Article 21.\nThe landmark judgment was Justice K.S. Puttaswamy (Retd.) v. Union of India (2017).\nThe Court held that privacy is intrinsic to the freedoms and dignity protected by the Constitution.","revisionPoints":"Right to Privacy → Article 21\nPuttaswamy judgment → 2017\nPrivacy is connected with:\nPersonal liberty\nHuman dignity\nAutonomy\nInformational privacy\nArticle 21: Protection of life and personal liberty."},
{"no":77,"text":"What are the duties of the Chief of Defence Staff (CDS) as Head of the Department of Military Affairs?\n1. Permanent Chairman of Chiefs of Staff Committee\n2. Exercise military command over the three Service Chiefs\n3. Principal Military Adviser to Defence Minister on all tri-service matters","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"D","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Chief of Defence Staff (CDS) is the permanent Chairman of the Chiefs of Staff Committee.\nThe CDS serves as the Principal Military Adviser to the Defence Minister on tri-service matters.\nHowever, the CDS does not exercise military command over the three Service Chiefs. The Army, Navy and Air Force Chiefs continue to exercise command over their respective services.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"CDS created: 2019.\nFirst CDS: General Bipin Rawat.\nCDS is:\nPermanent Chairman of Chiefs of Staff Committee.\nHead of Department of Military Affairs.\nPrincipal military adviser to Defence Minister on tri-service matters.\nCDS does not exercise command over the three service chiefs.\nA major objective is jointness and integration among the armed forces."},
{"no":78,"text":"Operations undertaken by the Army towards upliftment of the local population in remote areas to include addressing of their basic needs is called:","A":"Operation Sankalp","B":"Operation Maitri","C":"Operation Sadbhavana","D":"Operation Madad","ans":"C","category":"Science & Technology","subcategory":"Defence","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Operation Sadbhavana is an Indian Army initiative aimed at promoting goodwill and improving the quality of life of people in remote and border areas.\nIt includes activities such as:\nEducation\nMedical assistance\nInfrastructure development\nCommunity engagement\nSupport for basic needs\nThe initiative is particularly associated with remote areas, including regions affected by difficult terrain and socio-economic challenges.","revisionPoints":"Operation Sadbhavana → Indian Army\nMain objective → people-centric development and goodwill.\nActivities include:\nMedical camps\nEducational initiatives\nInfrastructure support\nCommunity development\nOperation Maitri: Associated with humanitarian/disaster relief operations, particularly in Nepal after the 2015 earthquake.\nOperation Sankalp: Indian Navy maritime security operation in the Persian Gulf/Arabian Sea context."},
{"no":79,"text":"The longest border between any two countries in the world is between:","A":"Canada and the United States of America","B":"Chile and Argentina","C":"China and India","D":"Kazakhstan and Russian Federation","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Canada–United States border is the longest international land border in the world.\nIt extends for approximately 8,891 km, including the Alaska–Canada section.\nThe border runs across a large part of North America.\nIt is notable for being a largely peaceful and demilitarised international boundary.","revisionPoints":"Longest international land border → Canada–USA\nApproximate length → 8,891 km\nThe border includes:\nCanada–contiguous USA boundary\nAlaska–Canada boundary\nIndia–Bangladesh: India's longest international land border.\nIndia–China: One of India's longest international boundaries, but not the world's longest."},
{"no":80,"text":"Which of the following statements about the Ethics Committee in the Lok Sabha are correct?\n1. Initially it was an ad-hoc Committee.\n2. Only a Member of the Lok Sabha can make a complaint relating to unethical conduct of a Member of the Lok Sabha.\n3. This Committee cannot take up any matter which is sub-judice.","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliamentary Committees","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct. The Ethics Committee of the Lok Sabha was initially constituted as an ad hoc committee and was later made a permanent standing committee.\nStatement 2 is incorrect. A complaint concerning unethical conduct can be made by a person other than a Member of the Lok Sabha, subject to the prescribed procedure and requirements.\nStatement 3 is correct. The Committee does not take up matters that are sub judice.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Ethics Committee: Deals with matters relating to ethical conduct of Lok Sabha members.\nInitially constituted as an ad hoc committee.\nSubsequently made a permanent committee.\nComplaints can originate from persons other than MPs, subject to procedural requirements.\nSub judice matters are outside its scope.\nEthics Committee is different from the Privileges Committee, which deals primarily with breaches of parliamentary privilege."},
{"no":81,"text":"Consider the following statements regarding 'Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam':\n1. Provisions will come into effect from the 18th Lok Sabha.\n2. This will be in force for 15 years after becoming an Act.\n3. There are provisions for the reservation of seats for Scheduled Caste Women within the quota reserved for the Scheduled Castes.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1 and 3 only","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam, formally the Constitution (106th Amendment) Act, 2023, provides for reservation of approximately one-third of seats for women in the Lok Sabha and State Legislative Assemblies.\nStatement 1 is incorrect. The reservation does not automatically begin with the 18th Lok Sabha. It will take effect after delimitation is undertaken following publication of the relevant census figures.\nStatement 2 is correct. The reservation is provided for a period of 15 years from the commencement of the relevant constitutional provisions, subject to continuation by parliamentary action.\nStatement 3 is correct. Within the seats reserved for Scheduled Castes, one-third are to be reserved for SC women.\nTherefore, Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Nari Shakti Vandan Adhiniyam → 106th Constitutional Amendment Act, 2023\nProvides one-third reservation for women in:\nLok Sabha\nState Legislative Assemblies\nDelhi Legislative Assembly\nReservation is linked to delimitation after the relevant census.\n15-year period is specified.\nSC/ST women: One-third of seats reserved for SC/ST are also reserved for women.\nIt does not provide reservation in the Rajya Sabha."},
{"no":82,"text":"Which of the following statements about 'Exercise Mitra Shakti-2023' are correct?\n1. This was a joint military exercise between India and Bangladesh.\n2. It commenced in Aundh (Pune).\n3. Joint response during counter-terrorism operations was a goal of this operation.\n4. Indian Air Force was a part of this exercise.","A":"1, 2 and 3","B":"1, 2 and 4","C":"1, 3 and 4","D":"2, 3 and 4","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Defence Cooperation","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Exercise Mitra Shakti-2023 was a joint military exercise between India and Sri Lanka, not Bangladesh.\nThe exercise commenced at Aundh Military Station, Pune.\nIt focused on enhancing interoperability and developing joint capabilities, particularly for counter-terrorism operations.\nThe Indian Air Force was also part of the exercise, making it a tri-service-oriented exercise.\nTherefore, Statements 2, 3 and 4 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Mitra Shakti → India–Sri Lanka\n2023 edition → Aundh Military Station, Pune\nFocus:\nJoint operations\nCounter-terrorism\nInteroperability\nHumanitarian assistance and disaster relief\nBangladesh → Exercise Sampriti is a major India-Bangladesh Army exercise.\nIndia–Sri Lanka → Mitra Shakti."},
{"no":83,"text":"A Writ of Prohibition is an order issued by the Supreme Court or High Courts to:","A":"A government officer prohibiting him from taking a particular action.","B":"The Parliament/Legislative Assembly to pass a law on Prohibition.","C":"The lower court prohibiting continuation of proceedings in a case.","D":"The Government prohibiting it from following an unconstitutional policy.","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Judiciary","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Writ of Prohibition is issued by a higher court to a lower court or tribunal when it is acting or about to act beyond its jurisdiction.\nIt prevents the lower judicial/quasi-judicial authority from continuing proceedings that exceed its jurisdiction.\nIt is therefore essentially a preventive writ.\nIt differs from Certiorari, which is generally used to quash an order or decision already made by a lower court or tribunal.","revisionPoints":"Prohibition → Prevents a lower court/tribunal from exceeding jurisdiction.\nCertiorari → Quashes an order of a lower court/tribunal.\nHabeas Corpus → Release from unlawful detention.\nMandamus → Commands a public authority to perform a public duty.\nQuo Warranto → Questions the legal authority of a person occupying a public office."},
{"no":84,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. It is the Governor of the State who recognizes and declares any community of the State as a Scheduled Tribe.\n2. A community declared as a Scheduled Tribe in a State need not be so in another State.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Tribes","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Under Article 342, the President specifies the tribes or tribal communities to be regarded as Scheduled Tribes in relation to a particular State or Union Territory, after consultation with the Governor where necessary.\nParliament can subsequently include or exclude tribes from the notified list.\nTherefore, the Governor does not independently declare a community as a Scheduled Tribe.\nStatement 2 is correct. A community may be recognised as a Scheduled Tribe in one State but not necessarily in another State.\nHence, only Statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Article 342 → Scheduled Tribes\nPresident: Specifies STs for a State/UT by public notification.\nParliament: Can include or exclude communities from the ST list.\nST status is generally State/UT-specific.\nArticle 341 → Scheduled Castes\nArticle 342A → Socially and Educationally Backward Classes"},
{"no":85,"text":"With reference to Union Budget, consider the following statements:\n1. The Union Finance Minister on behalf of the Prime Minister lays the Annual Financial Statement before both the Houses of Parliament.\n2. At the Union level, no demand for grant can be made except on the recommendation of the President of India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect. Under Article 112, the President causes the Annual Financial Statement to be laid before both Houses of Parliament. It is conventionally presented in Parliament by the Union Finance Minister, but it is not laid by the Finance Minister \"on behalf of the Prime Minister\" as stated.\nStatement 2 is correct. Under Article 113, no demand for a grant can be made except on the recommendation of the President.\nTherefore, only Statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"Article 112 → Annual Financial Statement\nArticle 113 → Demands for Grants\nArticle 114 → Appropriation Bills\nArticle 110 → Money Bill\nThe Annual Financial Statement shows estimated:\nReceipts\nExpenditureof the Government for the financial year.\nCharged expenditure is not submitted to vote, although it can be discussed."},
{"no":86,"text":"Who of the following is the author of the books The India Way and Why Bharat Matters?","A":"Bhupender Yadav","B":"Nalin Mehta","C":"Shashi Tharoor","D":"Subrahmanyam Jaishankar","ans":"D","category":"Art & Culture","subcategory":"Books & Authors","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Dr. Subrahmanyam Jaishankar, India's External Affairs Minister, authored both The India Way: Strategies for an Uncertain World and Why Bharat Matters.\nThe India Way discusses India's foreign-policy choices and strategies in a changing global order.\nWhy Bharat Matters examines India's role and interests in the contemporary international system.","revisionPoints":"The India Way → S. Jaishankar\nWhy Bharat Matters → S. Jaishankar\nKey themes:\nIndian foreign policy\nStrategic autonomy\nMultipolarity\nIndia's rise\nChanging global order\nShashi Tharoor: Author of several books on Indian history, politics and society."},
{"no":87,"text":"Consider the following pairs:\nCountry\nReason for being in the news\n1. Argentina\nWorst economic crisis\n2. Sudan\nWar between the country's regular army and paramilitary forces\n3. Turkey\nRescinded its membership of NATO\nHow many of the above pairs are correctly matched?","A":"Only one pair","B":"Only two pairs","C":"All three pairs","D":"None of the pairs","ans":"B","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Argentina — Worst economic crisis: Correct in the context of the severe economic crisis, including very high inflation, currency pressures and fiscal difficulties.\nSudan — War between regular army and paramilitary forces: Correct. The conflict involved the Sudanese Armed Forces (SAF) and the Rapid Support Forces (RSF).\nTurkey — Rescinded its NATO membership: Incorrect. Turkey remains a NATO member.\nTherefore, Pairs 1 and 2 are correctly matched.","revisionPoints":"Sudan conflict: SAF vs RSF.\nRSF: Powerful paramilitary organisation in Sudan.\nTurkey: NATO member since 1952.\nArgentina has experienced recurring:\nInflation\nCurrency crises\nDebt problems\nFiscal instability\nUPSC often tests international events through country–event matching."},
{"no":88,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:Suez (Sumed) pipeline is a strategic route for Persian Gulf oil and natural gas shipments to Europe.\nII. Statement-II:Sumed pipeline connects the Red Sea with the Mediterranean Sea.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"A","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The SUMED (Suez–Mediterranean) pipeline is located in Egypt and provides an alternative route for transporting petroleum between the Red Sea and Mediterranean regions.\nIt is strategically important for transporting oil from the Persian Gulf toward European and Mediterranean markets.\nThe pipeline runs from Ain Sokhna on the Gulf of Suez to Sidi Kerir on the Mediterranean coast.\nBy providing a land-based connection between the two seas, it can reduce dependence on tanker passage through the Suez Canal for certain oil shipments.\nTherefore, both statements are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","revisionPoints":"SUMED → Suez–Mediterranean Pipeline\nLocation → Egypt\nConnects:\nGulf of Suez/Red Sea region\nMediterranean Sea\nStrategic importance → Persian Gulf oil transportation toward Europe.\nSuez Canal and SUMED pipeline are distinct transport routes."},
{"no":89,"text":"Consider the following statements:\n1. The Red Sea receives very little precipitation in any form.\n2. No water enters the Red Sea from rivers.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"C","category":"Geography","subcategory":"World Geography","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Red Sea lies in a highly arid region and receives very little rainfall.\nThere are no major perennial rivers discharging into the Red Sea.\nIts water balance is therefore strongly influenced by:\nHigh evaporation\nLimited precipitation\nExchange of water with the Gulf of Aden through the Bab-el-Mandeb Strait.\nHence, both statements are correct.","revisionPoints":"Red Sea → Highly arid environment\nVery high evaporation.\nMajor water exchange occurs through Bab-el-Mandeb Strait.\nRed Sea lies between:\nAfrica to the west\nArabian Peninsula to the east\nSuez Canal connects the Red Sea region with the Mediterranean Sea."},
{"no":90,"text":"According to the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA), which one of the following is the largest source of sulphur dioxide emissions?","A":"Locomotives using fossil fuels","B":"Ships using fossil fuels","C":"Extraction of metals from ores","D":"Power plants using fossil fuels","ans":"D","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Pollution","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Sulphur dioxide (SO₂) is primarily produced when sulphur-containing fossil fuels are burned.\nFossil-fuel-based power plants, particularly those using coal and high-sulphur fuels, have historically been a major source of SO₂ emissions.\nSO₂ contributes to:\nAcid rain\nFine particulate matter formation\nRespiratory health problems\nEcosystem damage\nTherefore, among the options, power plants using fossil fuels are the largest source.","revisionPoints":"SO₂: Major air pollutant associated with combustion of sulphur-containing fuels.\nMajor effects:\nAcid rain\nRespiratory irritation\nFormation of sulphate particulate matter\nMajor source → Fossil-fuel combustion, especially coal-fired power generation.\nSO₂ + atmospheric water/oxidants → sulphuric acid and sulphate compounds\nNOₓ is another major precursor of acid deposition and contributes significantly to ground-level ozone formation."},
{"no":91,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:There is instability and worsening security situation in the Sahel region.\nII. Statement-II:There have been military takeovers/coups d'état in several countries of the Sahel region in the recent past.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"A","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Regional Affairs","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Sahel region of Africa has experienced significant political instability and deterioration in security conditions.\nSeveral countries have witnessed military coups, including Mali, Burkina Faso and Niger.\nThe region has also faced challenges from:\nTerrorist and extremist groups\nInsurgencies\nWeak governance\nPolitical instability\nInter-state tensions\nThe repeated military takeovers have contributed to political instability and weakened regional security arrangements.\nTherefore, both statements are correct, and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","revisionPoints":"Sahel: Semi-arid belt immediately south of the Sahara Desert.\nImportant countries include:\nMali\nBurkina Faso\nNiger\nChad\nMauritania\nMajor security concerns:\nIslamist militancy\nTerrorism\nPolitical instability\nCoups\nMali, Burkina Faso and Niger experienced military takeovers in the recent period.\nSahel is strategically important because it connects North Africa with Sub-Saharan Africa."},
{"no":92,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:India does not import apples from the United States of America.\nII. Statement-II:In India, the law prohibits the import of Genetically Modified food without the approval of the competent authority.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"D","category":"Economy","subcategory":"International Trade","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"India does import apples from the United States. Therefore, Statement-I is incorrect.\nIndia regulates the import of genetically modified organisms and genetically modified foods.\nImport of GM food requires approval from the competent regulatory authority under India's regulatory framework.\nTherefore, Statement-II is correct.\nSince Statement-I is incorrect and Statement-II is correct, the answer is (d).","revisionPoints":"India imports apples from several countries, including the USA.\nGM foods are subject to regulatory approval in India.\nGenetic Engineering Appraisal Committee (GEAC) plays an important role in India's biosafety regulatory framework.\nGM crops/foods are different from:\nHybrid varieties\nConventionally bred varieties\nGenome-edited varieties\nUPSC may connect GM food regulation with biosafety and environmental regulation."},
{"no":93,"text":"With reference to the Speaker of the Lok Sabha, consider the following statements:\nWhile any resolution for the removal of the Speaker of the Lok Sabha is under consideration:\n1. He/She shall not preside.\n2. He/She shall not have the right to speak.\n3. He/She shall not be entitled to vote on the resolution in the first instance.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2 only","C":"2 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Under Article 181, when a resolution for the removal of the Speaker is under consideration in the Legislative Assembly, the Speaker shall not preside.\nThe corresponding constitutional principle for the Lok Sabha is contained in Article 96.\nThe Speaker can speak in the proceedings concerning the removal resolution.\nThe Speaker can also vote in the first instance as an ordinary member. However, the Speaker does not have a casting vote in such a situation.\nTherefore:\nStatement 1 → Correct\nStatement 2 → Incorrect\nStatement 3 → Incorrect\nHence, the answer is (a).","revisionPoints":"Article 93: Speaker and Deputy Speaker of Lok Sabha.\nArticle 94: Vacation/resignation/removal of Speaker and Deputy Speaker.\nArticle 95: Deputy Speaker's powers.\nArticle 96: Speaker/Deputy Speaker not to preside during consideration of their removal.\nSpeaker ordinarily has:\nA vote in the event of a tie (casting vote)\nAuthority to certify Money Bills\nRole in maintaining order and parliamentary procedure."},
{"no":94,"text":"With reference to the Indian Parliament, consider the following statements:\n1. A bill pending in the Lok Sabha lapses on its dissolution.\n2. A bill passed by the Lok Sabha and pending in the Rajya Sabha lapses on the dissolution of the Lok Sabha.\n3. A bill in regard to which the President of India notified his/her intention to summon the Houses to a joint sitting lapses on the dissolution of the Lok Sabha.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"B","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is correct. A Bill pending in the Lok Sabha lapses when the Lok Sabha is dissolved.\nStatement 2 is correct. A Bill passed by the Lok Sabha but pending in the Rajya Sabha also lapses upon dissolution of the Lok Sabha.\nStatement 3 is incorrect. A Bill does not lapse merely because the President has notified an intention to summon both Houses for a joint sitting under Article 108.\nTherefore, Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Dissolution affects only the Lok Sabha, not the Rajya Sabha.\nBills that generally lapse:\nBill pending in Lok Sabha.\nBill passed by Lok Sabha and pending in Rajya Sabha.\nBill pending in Rajya Sabha but not passed by Lok Sabha does not lapse.\nA Bill passed by both Houses and awaiting President's assent does not lapse.\nJoint sitting → Article 108.\nJoint sitting is presided over by the Speaker of Lok Sabha."},
{"no":95,"text":"With reference to the Parliament of India, consider the following statements:\n1. Prorogation of a House by the President does not require the advice of the Council of Ministers.\n2. Prorogation of a House is generally done after the House is adjourned sine die but there is no bar to the President of India proroguing the House which is in session.\n3. Dissolution of the Lok Sabha is done by the President of India who, save in exceptional circumstances, does so on the advice of the Council of Ministers.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"1 and 2","C":"2 and 3","D":"3 only","ans":"C","category":"Polity & Governance","subcategory":"Parliament","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The President exercises the power of prorogation under Article 85 on the advice of the Council of Ministers.\nStatement 2 is correct. Prorogation normally follows adjournment sine die, but the President can prorogue a House even while it is in session.\nStatement 3 is correct. The President dissolves the Lok Sabha, generally acting on the advice of the Council of Ministers.\nTherefore, Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"Summoning and prorogation → Article 85\nAdjournment: Done by the presiding officer; does not end the life of the House.\nProrogation: Ends a session of Parliament.\nDissolution: Ends the life of the Lok Sabha.\nRajya Sabha is a permanent House and is not dissolved.\nThe President generally acts on the aid and advice of the Council of Ministers."},
{"no":96,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:The European Parliament approved the Net-Zero Industry Act recently.\nII. Statement-II:The European Union intends to achieve carbon neutrality by 2040 and therefore aims to develop all of its own clean technology by that time.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"C","category":"Environment & Ecology","subcategory":"Climate Change","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Net-Zero Industry Act (NZIA) was approved by the European Parliament as part of the EU's effort to increase domestic manufacturing capacity for strategic net-zero technologies.\nThe Act aims to strengthen Europe's clean-technology manufacturing ecosystem and reduce dependence on external suppliers.\nStatement-II is incorrect because the EU's legally established target is to achieve climate neutrality by 2050, not 2040.\nThe NZIA does not mean that the EU intends to develop all of its clean technology domestically by that date.\nTherefore, only Statement-I is correct.","revisionPoints":"EU climate neutrality target → 2050\n2030 target: At least 55% reduction in net greenhouse-gas emissions compared with 1990 levels.\nNet-Zero Industry Act: Supports domestic production of strategic clean technologies.\nExamples:\nSolar technologies\nWind technologies\nBatteries\nHeat pumps\nElectrolysers\nCarbon capture technologies\nImportant distinction:\nClimate neutrality → 2050\nNot 2040"},
{"no":97,"text":"Consider the following statements:\nI. Statement-I:Recently, Venezuela has achieved a rapid recovery from its economic crisis and succeeded in preventing its people from fleeing/emigrating to other countries.\nII. Statement-II:Venezuela has the world's largest oil reserves.\nWhich one of the following is correct?","A":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct and Statement-II explains Statement-I.","B":"Both Statement-I and Statement-II are correct, but Statement-II does not explain Statement-I.","C":"Statement-I is correct, but Statement-II is incorrect.","D":"Statement-I is incorrect, but Statement-II is correct.","ans":"D","category":"International Relations","subcategory":"Countries in News","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement-I is incorrect. Venezuela has experienced periods of economic stabilisation and recovery, but it has continued to face severe economic and humanitarian challenges, including large-scale migration.\nMillions of Venezuelans have left the country because of economic hardship, political instability and humanitarian conditions.\nStatement-II is correct. Venezuela possesses the world's largest proven crude oil reserves.\nTherefore, the correct answer is (d).","revisionPoints":"Venezuela → World's largest proven crude oil reserves.\nMajor oil-producing region → Orinoco Belt.\nDespite huge reserves, Venezuela has faced:\nEconomic crisis\nHyperinflation\nPolitical instability\nSanctions\nLarge-scale migration\nUPSC concept: Natural-resource abundance does not necessarily guarantee economic prosperity."},
{"no":98,"text":"With reference to the Digital India Land Records Modernisation Programme, consider the following statements:\n1. To implement the scheme, the Central Government provides 100% funding.\n2. Under the Scheme, Cadastral Maps are digitised.\n3. An initiative has been undertaken to transliterate the Records of Rights from local language to any one of the languages recognized by the Constitution of India.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 and 2 only","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"1 and 3 only","D":"1, 2 and 3","ans":"A","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"The Digital India Land Records Modernisation Programme (DILRMP) is a centrally sponsored scheme aimed at modernising land records and improving transparency in land administration.\nStatement 1 is correct: The Centre provides 100% funding for the major components of the programme.\nStatement 2 is correct: Digitisation of cadastral maps is an important component.\nStatement 3 is incorrect: The programme includes computerisation and integration of land records, but the statement regarding mandatory transliteration into any one of the constitutionally recognised languages is not a prescribed feature in the manner stated.\nHence, Statements 1 and 2 are correct.","revisionPoints":"DILRMP → Department of Land Resources, Ministry of Rural Development\nObjectives:\nComputerisation of land records\nDigitisation of cadastral maps\nIntegration of textual and spatial records\nModernisation of registration\nReduction of land disputes\nCadastral map: Map showing boundaries and parcels of land.\nDigital land records improve:\nTransparency\nAccessibility\nSecurity of ownership records\nEase of land transactions"},
{"no":99,"text":"With reference to the 'Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan', consider the following statements:\n1. This scheme guarantees a minimum package of antenatal care services to women in their second and third trimesters of pregnancy and six months post-delivery health care service in any government health facility.\n2. Under this scheme, private sector health care providers of certain specialities can volunteer to provide services at nearby government health facilities.\nWhich of the statements given above is/are correct?","A":"1 only","B":"2 only","C":"Both 1 and 2","D":"Neither 1 nor 2","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Pradhan Mantri Surakshit Matritva Abhiyan (PMSMA) aims to provide assured, quality antenatal care to pregnant women.\nStatement 1 is incorrect. PMSMA provides assured antenatal care, particularly during the second and third trimesters, but the statement incorrectly adds a guaranteed six-month post-delivery healthcare service as part of the scheme.\nStatement 2 is correct. Private-sector doctors and healthcare professionals can voluntarily participate and provide services at designated government health facilities.\nTherefore, only Statement 2 is correct.","revisionPoints":"PMSMA launched → 2016\nMinistry → Ministry of Health and Family Welfare\nFocus → Antenatal care\nSpecial focus on:\nSecond and third trimesters\nIdentification of high-risk pregnancies\nAppropriate referral and management\nPrivate-sector specialists can volunteer to provide services at government facilities.\nImportant objective → reducing maternal and neonatal mortality."},
{"no":100,"text":"With reference to the Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan-dhan (PM-SYM) Yojana, consider the following statements:\n1. The entry age group for enrolment in the scheme is 21 to 40 years.\n2. Age-specific contribution shall be made by the beneficiary.\n3. Each subscriber under the scheme shall receive a minimum pension of ₹3,000 per month after attaining the age of 60 years.\n4. Family pension is applicable to spouse and unmarried daughters.\nWhich of the statements given above are correct?","A":"1, 3 and 4","B":"2 and 3 only","C":"2 only","D":"1, 2 and 4","ans":"B","category":"Economy","subcategory":"Government Schemes","tags":["UPSC","2024"],"expl":"Statement 1 is incorrect. The entry age for PM-SYM is 18 to 40 years, not 21 to 40 years.\nStatement 2 is correct. The beneficiary makes an age-specific monthly contribution, with the Central Government making a matching contribution.\nStatement 3 is correct. On attaining 60 years of age, an eligible subscriber receives a minimum assured pension of ₹3,000 per month.\nStatement 4 is incorrect. On the death of the subscriber after commencement of pension, the spouse is entitled to 50% of the pension as family pension. The provision is not a family pension for both the spouse and unmarried daughters.\nTherefore, Statements 2 and 3 are correct.","revisionPoints":"PM-SYM → Pradhan Mantri Shram Yogi Maan-dhan\nTarget group → Unorganised workers\nEntry age → 18–40 years\nMinimum assured pension → ₹3,000/month\nPension begins at → 60 years\nGovernment provides a matching contribution.\nBeneficiary contribution varies according to entry age.\nSpouse is entitled to 50% of the pension as family pension after the subscriber's death."},
];
